From 4f98d886e3b6f6f431562c81bd27e0899c2f6c99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:54:17 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 49/56] super-amd: add disks to a RAID0

mdadm --grow <RAID0> --raid-devices=N is recorded as the AMD driver
records the migration (rcadm -T -r0): the volume's LD with NORMAL, its
elements matched (0x10000), and the RAID0 of N it becomes, its elements
on the extents of the old (0x30000) and on spares of the container
(0x40000), the data of each moving from +0x5000 down to +0x800.  md
takes the RAID0 over as a RAID4 without its parity, as Grow.c does for
its own RAID0s, reshapes it held behind the transform log as a RAID5
getting disks, and makes it a RAID0 again once mdmon has written the
"100 %" and the final packet.  mdmon, which does not run for a container
of RAID0s, is started for it.  A RAID0 midway through such a migration,
ours or the AMD driver's, is assembled as that RAID4 reshaping from the
mark of its log, its first new member in sync up to there.

The region a transform copies at a time is now worked out from both
LDs, as RC_TransformProtectSize does: 0x7e00 for a RAID0 of 2 getting a
disk, in whole stripes of both - not of the new LD only.

The AMD driver's second migration of a volume whose data moved already
(a RAID0 of 3 given a fourth disk, the data at +0x800 stay) md goes on
with in place, its offsets kept, each region as large as the new layout
can take below the row of the old one at the mark, as the AMD driver
works it out (RC_TransformUpdateNextDynamicRegionFromBeginning).  One
still in its first stripes, which the AMD driver moves through a log of
their data, is not assembled; one stopped there before anything moved
runs as it was, the migration left recorded (mdmon does not run for a
RAID0), and mdadm --grow drops it and starts anew.  mdadm starts no
such migration.

Tested on an AMD X870E board: a RAID0 of 2 SSDs given a HDD - the data
the same, the BIOS menu shows it Normal with the new capacity, the AMD
Windows driver reads the same data; one of ours stopped midway, the
Windows driver finished itself; one stopped before its first region md
went on with from 0 at the next assembly.  With rcraid 9.3.3 in a QEMU
guest: either finishes the other's migration stopped midway, and md
finishes the AMD driver's first and second ones, the data those rcraid
finished.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
